AB  - Purpose: To evaluate manganese (Mn2+)-enhanced MRI (MEMRI) and diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) as tools for detection of axonal injury and regeneration after intravitreal peripheral nerve graft (PNG) implantation in the rat optic nerve (ON).
   Materials and Methods: In adult Fischer rats, retinal ganglion cell (RGC) survival was evaluated in Flurogold (FG) back-filled retinal whole mounts after ON crush (ONC), intravitreal PNG, and intravitreal MnCl2 injection (150 nmol) at 0 and 20 days post lesion (dpl). MEMRI and echoplanar 137171 (DTI-EPI) was obtained of noninjured ON one day after intravitreal MnCl2 injection, and at 1 and 21 dpl after ONC, intravitreal PNG, and intravitreal MnCl2 injections given at 0 and 20 dpl. GAP-43 immunohistochemistry was performed after the last MRI.
   Results: ONC reduced RGC density in retina by 94% at 21 dpl compared to noninjured ON without MnCl2 injections. Both intravitreal PNG and intravitreal MnCl2 injections improved RGC survival in retina, which was reduced by 90% (ONC + MnCl2), 82% (ONC + PNG), and 74% (ONC+PNG+MnCl2) compared to noninjured ON. DTI-derived parameters [fractional anisotropy [FA], mean diffusivity, axial diffusivity lambda(parallel to) and radial diffusivity lambda(1)) were unaffected by the presence of Mn2+ in the ON. At 1 dpl, CNRMEMRI and lambda(parallel to) were reduced at the injury site, while at 21 dp1 they were increased at the injury site compared to values measured at I dpl. GAP-43 immunoreactive axons were present in the ON distal to the ONC injury site.
   Conclusion: MEMRI and DTI enabled detection of functional and structural degradation after rat ON injury, and there was correlation between the MRI-derived and immunohistochemical measures of axon regeneration.
